import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Authorship"
  description="Who counts as an author, how order is decided, and what the contributions statement has to say."
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="Publication"
    title="Authorship"
    lede="A byline is a claim about who did the work. It is worth getting right before it becomes permanent."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Who is an author</h2>
      <p>
        Someone who contributed to the design, the execution, the analysis, or the writing,
        and who takes responsibility for the result. A person who lent equipment, gave
        advice, or supervised is acknowledged in the contributions statement rather than
        listed as an author. Both of those are honest positions; only one of them is a
        byline.
      </p>

      <h2>Adding a co-author is an invitation</h2>
      <p>
        You name someone, and they receive an invitation they have to accept. Nothing is
        submitted while an invitation is outstanding. This exists so that work is never
        attributed to a person who did not agree to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Removing a co-author always requires approval from an officer, at every stage, and
        the person removed is always told. That holds even before the roster locks.
      </p>

      <h2>Author order</h2>
      <p>
        Order is set on its own screen, with the byline previewed as it will appear, and it
        has to be saved explicitly. Any current author can change it and everyone is
        notified when they do.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each competition sets a date after which the roster locks. At that point the entry
        stores a snapshot of who was registered, because the team that competed is a
        historical fact. Changes after the lock need an officer and the advisor, with the
        reason recorded.
      </p>

      <h2>The contributions statement</h2>
      <p>
        Every published record carries one. It names what each author did and what any
        mentor did, in plain sentences. It is required, it is checked against the list of
        authors, and a reviewer is asked whether it matches what the paper describes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Understating a mentor's role is the mistake to avoid. Experienced judges detect it,
        and they treat it as a question of integrity rather than presentation. A statement
        that says clearly what you did yourself is the strongest version of this section,
        and it is the same evidence that independent work is scored on.
      </p>

      <h2>Co-authors outside this organization</h2>
      <p>
        They appear on the byline as plain text, with their school, and they do not get an
        author page. They never agreed to a permanent page here and would have no way to
        control one.
      </p>

      <h2>After you leave</h2>
      <p>
        Your author page and your published records stay. If you archive your author page,
        the records stay published and your name becomes plain text on them, because they
        belong to your co-authors too. Removing a published record is never a decision one
        author makes alone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
